                "id": "cheque-de-garantie-criminal-law-warning",
                "heading": "Legal Audit: The 'Chèque de Garantie' Trap & Moroccan Penal Code Article 544",
                "content": "When researching car rentals in Morocco on forums or speaking with unverified street-level agencies in Gueliz (Marrakech), Maarif (Casablanca), or Agadir's tourist sector, you may encounter agents who ask for either a blank signed cheque or a blank imprint of your debit card details written on paper.\n\nAs a forensic automotive auditor in Morocco, I must be absolute on this point: **Never sign an undated cheque, a promissory note (*Reconnaissance de dette*), or a paper credit/debit slip without a printed numerical cap.**\n\n### The Legal Framework: Article 544 of the Moroccan Penal Code\nIn Morocco, the *Chèque de Garantie* (security deposit cheque) is **explicitly illegal** under **Article 544 of the Moroccan Penal Code (*Code Pénal Marocain*)** and **Article 316 of the Commercial Code (*Code de Commerce*)**.\n- Under Moroccan law, a cheque is exclusively an instrument of immediate payment (*instrument de paiement à vue*), never an instrument of credit or security.\n- Paradoxically, while accepting a security cheque is a crime for the rental agency (punishable by 1 to 5 years imprisonment and a fine of 2,000 to 10,000 MAD), **issuing a cheque without matching liquid funds (*chèque sans provision*)** remains a strict criminal offense for the driver if the agency attempts to cash it at a bank branch.\n\n### Passport Confiscation is Strictly Illegal\nSome unverified budget garages in Tangier or Fez will offer to \"waive the credit card deposit\" if you leave your original passport or National ID card (*CIN*) with them for the duration of the rental.\n- **Why you must refuse:** Leaving your original passport violates Moroccan security protocols. On national highways and entries to every city (especially routes heading toward Chefchaouen, Essaouira, or Ouarzazate), the **Gendarmerie Royale** and **DGSN (Police)** operate permanent checkpoints (*Barrages Judiciaires*).\n- Non-resident drivers are legally required to present three physical originals upon request: **1. Original Passport (showing entry stamp), 2. Original Driving License, and 3. Original Car Rental Contract (*Contrat de Location*) + Insurance Certificate**. Presenting a smartphone photo of your passport because a rental agency confiscated the original can result in vehicle immobilization (*Fourrière*) and hours of interrogation at the local brigade.\n\n### How to Properly Document a Cash or Debit Deposit\nBefore handing over a single Dirham or Euro note for a no-credit-card deposit, verify that the paper or digital **Contrat de Location** contains a dedicated box labeled **Caution / Dépôt de Garantie**. Ensure the agent writes the exact amount and currency: e.g., *\"Caution reçue en espèces : 400 Euros (Quatre cents Euros) - Restituable au retour du véhicule\"*, accompanied by the company's official ink stamp (*Cachet de l'entreprise*) and the agent's signature.",
