                "id": "step-by-step-handover-inspection-protocol",
                "heading": "The 6-Step Handover Protocol to Guarantee 100% Return of Your Cash/Debit Deposit",
                "content": "When you rent with a cash deposit or direct debit charge, the dynamic changes at vehicle return: instead of an automated bank release, a human agent must physically hand back your banknotes or trigger a terminal refund. Following a rigorous forensic handover protocol eliminates 99% of return disputes.\n\n### Step 1: The Continuous 4K Video Walkaround (With Flash)\nAirport underground parking garages (especially CMN Terminal 1/2 and RAK car parks) have notoriously dim lighting designed to hide micro-scratches. Turn on your smartphone's LED flash and record a **single, unbroken 2-minute video** before loading your luggage:\n- Start by filming the license plate and the rental agent standing next to the car (this proves timestamp and location).\n- Crouch down to film the **front lower bumper lip (*bas de caisse*)**—almost every rental car in Morocco has curb scrapes underneath the front bumper from high Moroccan sidewalks.\n- Film all four wheel rims/hubcaps up close, the windshield (looking from inside out against the light to spot filled star-chips), and the roof.\n\n### Step 2: Check the Spare Tire & Jack Kit (*Roue de Secours et Cric*)\nOpen the trunk and lift the carpet mat. Verify the presence of:\n1. An inflated spare tire (*roue de secours*) or complete inflation kit.\n2. The vehicle jack (*cric*) and wheel wrench (*clé démonte-roue*).\n3. The high-visibility yellow vest (*gilet fluorescent*) and warning triangle (*triangle de pré-signalisation*)—both are mandatory at Gendarmerie checkpoints; missing them triggers a **300 MAD on-the-spot fine**.\n\n### Step 3: Fuel Gauge & AdBlue Verification\nMoroccan agencies rarely use the European \"Full-to-Full\" (*Plein à Plein*) policy. Instead, they use **\"Same-to-Same\" (*Niveau pour Niveau*)**. If the digital gauge on your Dacia Sandero or Duster shows 4 out of 8 bars, photograph the dashboard showing both the exact **Odometer mileage** and the **Fuel bars** with the engine running. If driving a diesel Duster or Clio 5, ensure no **AdBlue warning light** is illuminated on the cluster; if AdBlue runs dry mid-trip, the engine computer will prevent the vehicle from restarting.\n\n### Step 4: Insist on Contract Diagram Markings\nNever accept the verbal phrase *\"Don't worry, we know about those scratches, it's normal in Morocco.\"* Politely insist that the agent marks every existing scratch, dent, and seat stain on the vehicle diagram (*État des lieux du véhicule*) on both carbon copies of the contract before you sign.\n\n### Step 5: Pre-Return Car Wash (*Lavage Extérieur*)\nIf you return a car caked in red clay from Ouarzazate or salt spray from Essaouira, the agent cannot inspect the paintwork and may hold back **300 to 500 MAD** pending a wash. Stop at any local TotalEnergies, Afriquia, or Shell station 20 minutes before return and pay **30 to 50 MAD (€3 to €4.50)** for an exterior pressure wash (*Lavage simple*). \n\n### Step 6: Departure Buffer at the Airport\nWhen recovering a cash deposit at the airport drop-off zone, schedule your return **2 hours and 45 minutes before your flight**. Moroccan airports enforce security screening right at the terminal entrance doors, and giving the rental agent a calm 15-minute window to inspect the car ensures you walk away with 100% of your cash deposit without rushing.",
