                "id": "legal-recourse-and-dispute-resolution",
                "heading": "What to Do During an Active Return Dispute: Legal Framework & Chargeback Strategy",
                "content": "Imagine it is 6:30 AM at Marrakech Menara Airport. Your flight boards in 90 minutes. The return attendant points to a pre-existing scuff under the bumper and demands 4,500 MAD (€415) on the spot, threatening to withhold your deposit release or claiming they will call the police to block you at passport control.\n\n### 1. Debunking the 'Passport Control Block' Bluff\nFirst, understand Moroccan jurisdiction: a commercial dispute over a rental car scratch is a strictly civil contractual matter governed by the **Dahir formant Code des Obligations et des Contrats (D.O.C.)**. A car rental agent has zero authority to place you on a border watch list or stop you from clearing *Direction Générale de la Sûreté Nationale* (DGSN) passport control at the airport. Only a royal prosecutor (*Procureur du Roi*) can issue a border hold, and solely for severe criminal offenses (such as a hit-and-run involving bodily injury).\n\n### 2. The Immediate Counter-Move at the Desk\nStay calm and do not raise your voice. Pull out your phone and display the WhatsApp message you sent them on Day 1 containing the pickup video and timestamped undercarriage footage. State clearly:\n- *\"Under Article 31 of Moroccan Consumer Protection Law 31-08, any abusive claim without a contradictory damage report is void. I have timestamped 4K video proof sent to your number at pickup.\"* \n- If they refuse to back down, do **not** sign any document acknowledging damage (*Reconnaissance de Dommage* or *Devis de Réparation*). Instead, write on their return form: **\"Je conteste formellement ce dommage préexistant - Preuve vidéo horodatée fournie\"** (I formally contest this pre-existing damage - timestamped video proof provided), photograph the sheet, and walk into the terminal.\n\n### 3. Calling the Tourist Police (*Brigade Touristique*)\nIf an agent physically blocks your luggage, refuses to return a cash deposit, or attempts extortion inside an airport zone, do not argue. Dial **19** (National Police) from a local SIM or walk 100 meters to the airport police post and request the **Brigade Touristique** or airport *Sûreté Nationale*. Moroccan authorities take aggressive action against unlicensed touts and rogue car rental operators harassing foreign tourists at international entry points.\n\n### 4. Winning the Bank Chargeback (Visa Dispute Condition 13.1 / Mastercard 4853)\nIf the agency captures part or all of your credit card pre-authorization without your signature on a return damage slip, file a formal Chargeback with your issuing bank the moment it transitions from 'Pending' to 'Posted'. Provide your bank with:\n1. The signed pickup contract showing pre-existing marks.\n2. The WhatsApp screenshot proving the pickup video was transmitted at the start of the rental.\n3. The drop-off video showing identical condition.\n4. Proof that the agency never supplied an independent certified surveyor's invoice (*Rapport d'Expertise Assermenté*) or actual bodyshop tax invoice (*Facture avec ICE*), which Visa/Mastercard merchant rules mandate to justify damage captures when contested by the cardholder.",
