                "id": "uninsured-exclusions-bris-de-glace-pneumatiques-piste",
                "heading": "The Exclusion Zones: Glass, Tires, Undercarriage, and 'Piste' Driving",
                "content": "Even when upgrading to Zero Excess (*Franchise Zéro*), Moroccan insurance underwriting separates structural bodywork (*Carrosserie*) from high-wear environmental components. Unless specifically bundled into a premium package (often labeled **Pack VIP** or **SCDW + BGP**), four standard exclusions apply across 95% of Moroccan contracts.\n\n### 1. Bris de Glace (Windshield, Glass & Optics)\nThe N9 highway between Marrakech and Ouarzazate (Tizi n'Tichka pass) and the A7 motorway toward Agadir undergo constant rockfall mitigation and roadwork. Loose gravel kicked up by heavy Sinotruk dump trucks frequently causes star-cracks on windshields. \n- Replacement cost for an acoustic windshield with rain sensors on a **Hyundai Tucson Auto**: **4,200 to 5,800 MAD (€385–€535)**.\n- Always verify if your Zero Excess includes the **BG (Bris de Glace)** extension, which costs an extra **30 to 40 MAD (€3–€4)/day** and covers windshields, side windows, and headlight lenses.\n\n### 2. Pneumatiques & Jantes (Tires, Rims & Sidewalls)\nPotholes (*nids-de-poule*) on secondary R-roads (such as the R307 or R703 in the High Atlas) often feature sharp asphalt drop-offs at the shoulder. Dropping a right wheel off the tarmac at 70 km/h will pinch-flat the sidewall (*hernie sur le flanc*).\n- A single All-Terrain Continental or Michelin tire for a **Dacia Duster 4x4** or **Toyota Prado TX** costs **1,600 to 2,800 MAD (€145–€260)**.\n- Standard Zero Excess covers the bodywork if a blowout causes a collision, but **excludes the rubber tire itself** unless Tire & Glass protection (*Assurance Pneus & Glaces*) is explicitly ticked.\n\n### 3. Soubassement (Undercarriage & Oil Sump) & Unpaved Tracks (*Pistes*)\nEvery rental vehicle in Morocco—including 4x4s—is equipped with a real-time GPS/GSM telemetry module (typically Teltonika units tracking G-force impacts and exact coordinates). \n- **The Legal Rule:** All insurance coverage, including Zero Excess, applies strictly to mapped, paved roads (*voies goudronnées ou carrossables*).\n- If you take a 2WD crossover onto the rocky *piste* between Boumalne Dades and M'semrir (R704 unpaved section) or drive into the Erg Chebbi sand dunes at Merzouga, the GPS logs off-road coordinates. If you crack the aluminum oil pan (*carter d'huile*) or bend a control arm on a rock, all waivers are legally voided under the **Negligence & Off-Road Clause**.\n- Only when renting a dedicated 4WD vehicle like the **Dacia Duster 4x4** or **Toyota Prado TX 7-Places** with prior route authorization do reputable agencies maintain coverage on graded gravel tracks.",
