                "id": "decoding-moroccan-road-signs",
                "heading": "Decoding Moroccan Signage: Arabic, Tifinagh, French & Speed Traps",
                "content": "Moroccan road signage follows the 1968 Vienna Convention on Road Signs and Signals—meaning shapes and color codes mirror continental Europe (red circles for prohibitions, blue circles for mandatory actions, red triangles for hazards). However, directional and regulatory legends are trilingual, written in **Classical Arabic**, **Amazigh (Tifinagh script)**, and **French**.\n\nWhile major *Autoroutes* (ADM toll highways) feature clear Latin script well in advance of exits, rural secondary roads (*Routes Régionales* and *Routes Provinciales*) and historic city centers present three major navigational hazards for foreign tourists.\n\n### 1. The 'Qif' (قف) Stop Sign\nOlder octagonal red STOP signs in rural provinces, the High Atlas, and urban medina perimeters display only the Arabic script **قف** (*Qif*). Regardless of whether the Latin letters 'STOP' appear, the red octagon mandates a **complete mechanical halt** where the vehicle's wheels stop rotating for a full two seconds behind the white line. Rolling through a *Qif* sign at 3 km/h is one of the top revenue generators for municipal police (*Sûreté Nationale*), triggering a Class 1 fine of 400 MAD.\n\n### 2. The Descending Speed Cascade (100 → 80 → 60 → 40)\nWhen driving on National Routes (such as the N8 between Marrakech and Beni Mellal, or the N9 over Tizi n'Tichka), entering any rural commune or approaching a Gendarmerie checkpoint triggers a rapid, aggressive speed step-down. Within 400 meters, signs will drop from 100 km/h to 80, then 60, and finally 40 or 20 km/h at the checkpoint barrier.\n* **The Trap:** Foreign drivers typically coast gently by lifting off the accelerator, still traveling at 72 km/h as they pass the 60 km/h sign. Gendarmerie officers position portable tripod laser guns (TruCAM II) **30 meters behind the 60 km/h sign**. You must actively brake *before* passing the sign plane.\n\n### 3. Missing 'End of Restriction' Signs\nOn secondary roads, roadworks or hazard zones frequently post a temporary 40 km/h limit sign, but the crew never installs the white-and-black diagonal *Fin de limitation* (End of speed limit) sign. Legally, under Moroccan law, a speed restriction placed before a specific hazard or intersection resets **after you clear that intersection**. However, if the sign simply stands on an open stretch of rural road, maintain the reduced speed for at least 1.5 kilometers or until the next major crossroads before resuming 80 or 100 km/h.\n\n### Essential Road Terminology Glossary\n* **Ralentir / خفف السرعة:** Slow down immediately (indicates an imminent police checkpoint, radar, or sharp hairpin).\n* **Chaussée Déformée:** Deformed/sunken tarmac. Slow down to 50 km/h; hitting a subsided culvert at 90 km/h in a compact hatchback will rupture your oil pan.\n* **Radier Submersible:** Floodable concrete river crossing (common in the Draa and Dades valleys). Never cross if water obscures the concrete edges.\n* **Priorité à Droite:** Yield to all vehicles entering from right-hand side streets.",
