                "id": "urban-vs-rural-hazard-matrix",
                "heading": "The Unwritten Street Code: Navigating Urban Chaos & Rural Night Hazards",
                "content": "Understanding the statutory *Code de la Route* is only half the equation; surviving unscathed requires adapting to Morocco's informal kinetic street dynamics. Local driving is neither aggressive nor malicious—it is **fluid and nose-first**. Drivers expect you to react exclusively to what is inside your 180-degree forward windshield cone.\n\n### Urban Survival: Casablanca, Marrakech & Fes\n* **The Scooter Swarm (Marrakech & Casa):** In Marrakech, 50cc and 110cc mopeds (*C90s*) ignore lane markings entirely, filtering simultaneously on both your left and right flanks. **Rule:** Never make a sudden lateral steering correction to dodge a pothole inside a city without checking both wing mirrors. Hold a predictable, dead-straight line in your lane; the scooters will flow around you like water around a rock.\n* **Petits Taxis (Urban Taxis):** Painted red in Casablanca/Fes, ochre in Marrakech, and blue in Rabat. They are legally permitted to stop anywhere on a boulevard to pick up a hailing passenger. Never tailgate a Petit Taxi in the right-hand lane; leave a minimum 2-car buffer, as they will slam their brakes without engaging a turn signal the instant a pedestrian raises a hand on the sidewalk.\n* **Lane Splitting (2 Lanes Become 4):** Wide urban avenues frequently lose their painted lane lines. Keep your nose slightly offset from the car ahead so you cannot be boxed in, and yield peacefully if another car inserts its front bumper 30 centimeters ahead of yours.\n\n### Rural & Mountain Survival: The Atlas & Pre-Sahara\n* **Narrow Tarmac Shoulders (*Pistes Mixtes*):** Secondary roads in the south (e.g., around Ouarzazate, Zagora, or Chefchaouen backroads) sometimes feature a single central strip of asphalt barely 4 meters wide with rough gravel drops (*accotements*) on either side. When an oncoming truck or bus approaches, decelerate to 40 km/h, place your **right two wheels smoothly onto the gravel shoulder**, let the heavy vehicle pass, and steer gently back onto the tarmac without jerking the wheel (which causes gravel spin-outs).\n* **Triporteurs & Unlit Carts:** Three-wheeled cargo motorcycles (*Triporteurs*) and donkey carts operate heavily between rural *souks* (markets). They struggle up gradients at 15 km/h and almost never have functioning rear tail lights. This is the primary reason why **driving outside toll highways after sunset is strongly discouraged**. An unlit dark-grey Triporteur or stray dromedary is invisible against asphalt until it is 25 meters inside your low-beam cone.",
