                "answer": "No, if your domestic driver's license is printed in the Latin alphabet (such as licenses from the EU, UK, USA, Canada, or Australia) and you are staying as a tourist for less than one year (with a valid passport entry stamp under 90 days). Under the 1968 Vienna Convention, your original physical plastic license is 100% legally valid at all police and Gendarmerie checkpoints. An IDP (1949 or 1968 convention) is only mandatory if your license is printed exclusively in non-Latin script (e.g., Chinese, Cyrillic, Japanese, or Greek). Digital licenses on smartphone apps are strictly rejected by Moroccan police."
