                "id": "visual-choreography-barrage-stop-protocol",
                "heading": "The Visual Choreography of a 'Barrage': Ralentir vs. Halte Protocol",
                "content": "The vast majority of traffic fines issued to tourists at Moroccan checkpoints do not happen because of pre-existing infractions; they occur **inside the checkpoint zone itself** due to a failure to read the sign sequence. Fixed checkpoints (*Barrages Fixes*) at city entrances and provincial borders follow a rigid spatial layout governed by the Moroccan Highway Code.\n\n### The 4-Stage Approach Sequence\n\n1. **Stage 1: The De-escalation Zone (200m to 100m out):**\n   You will see sequential temporary signs on the right shoulder Stepping down the speed limit: **80 km/h -> 60 km/h -> 40 km/h**. You must actively brake, not merely coast. Gendarmes frequently position an optical laser unit *inside* the checkpoint tent to measure your approach speed inside the 60 km/h funnel.\n2. **Stage 2: The Chicane & Spike Strip (50m out):**\n   The road narrows into a single lane flanked by red-and-white metal barriers and a heavy steel spike strip (*herse*) laid across the asphalt. The speed limit here drops to **20 km/h**. Cross the metal housing of the spike strip at a crawl (10–15 km/h) in first or second gear to avoid damaging rental tires.\n3. **Stage 3: The 'Ralentir' vs. 'Halte' Decision Point:**\n   At the center of the roadblock stands a portable tripod sign. You must read the exact wording:\n   * **RALENTIR (Slow Down):** Keep the vehicle moving at walking pace (10 km/h). Maintain eye contact with the primary officer standing on the shoulder.\n   * **HALTE POLICE / قف الدرك الملكي (Stop Gendarmerie):** You must bring the vehicle to a **complete mechanical stop** two meters before the sign. Your wheels must achieve zero rotation for at least two full seconds.\n4. **Stage 4: Nighttime Protocol & Visual Clearance:**\n   If approaching a checkpoint between dusk and dawn, you must **switch off your dipped headlights (feux de croisement) and keep only your position/parking lights (feux de position) on**, while turning **ON your interior cabin dome light**. This prevents blinding the officers and allows them to see the occupants' hands and faces immediately.\n\nNever accelerate out of a checkpoint until the officer gives you a clear physical gesture—either a circular forward wave of the hand during the day, or a horizontal sweep of a green/white flashlight wand at night. Executing a 'California rolling stop' at a *Halte* sign is legally classified under Moroccan law as running a red light/stop sign, triggering an immediate 700 MAD fine.",
                "callout": {
                    "type": "warning",
                    "text": "Never photograph, film, or leave a dashboard camera (Dashcam) actively pointed at officers during a checkpoint approach. Recording military or police installations (including mobile Gendarmerie roadblocks) is a severe national security offense in Morocco. If you use a dashcam, unplug it and dismount it before entering Morocco."
                }
