                "id": "radar-technology-mesta-fusion-jumelles",
                "heading": "Hardware on the Asphalt: Mesta Fusion Towers, TruCAM Lasers & Checkpoint Traps",
                "content": "To avoid fines altogether, you must recognize the three distinct layers of speed enforcement deployed across major hubs like [Casablanca](/locations/casablanca), [Rabat](/locations/rabat), [Marrakech](/locations/marrakech), and [Tangier](/locations/tanger).\n\n### 1. Next-Gen Fixed Radars (Idemia Mesta Fusion 2)\nGone are the days of simple grey metal boxes that only flashed rear license plates in one lane. Since 2021, NARSA has deployed over 550 **Mesta Fusion 2** multi-target Doppler radar turrets—tall, sleek white-and-black vertical pillars mounted alongside highways and urban boulevards. \n* **Capabilities:** These towers track up to 32 vehicles simultaneously across 8 lanes at a range of 200 meters.\n* **Bi-Directional:** They photograph both **approaching (front)** and **receding (rear)** traffic simultaneously. Slowing down only when the radar is facing your side of the road will get you flashed from across the median on two-lane national roads.\n* **Multi-Infraction:** Beyond speed, high-resolution infrared cameras inside these pillars automatically detect vehicles running red lights, crossing solid white lane dividers, and driving on hard shoulders.\n\n### 2. Mobile Laser Binoculars (LTI TruCAM II & Ultralyte)\nOperated exclusively by the Royal Gendarmerie on national routes and highway interchanges, and by the DGSN inside cities, these tripod-mounted or handheld optical units are lethal to inattentive drivers.\n* **Line-of-Sight Range:** A Gendarme standing under the shade of an overpass or behind an oleander bush in the highway median can lock onto your front license plate from **600 to 1,000 meters away**—long before your eyes can spot their grey uniform.\n* **Photographic Proof:** Under Moroccan law, an officer cannot simply claim you were speeding based on visual estimation or an unrecorded radar gun. The modern TruCAM units record a high-definition video clip and still image stamped with GPS coordinates, date, time, target distance, measured speed, and retained speed. **You have the legal right to politely ask to view the screen (*voir la photo sur l'appareil*) to verify your license plate and recorded speed.**\n\n### 3. The Classic Moroccan Trap: The '60–80–60' Village Funnel\nThe single highest-grossing revenue trap in Morocco occurs on National Roads (like the N8 between Marrakech and Beni Mellal, or the N2 near Chefchaouen). As you cruise legally at 100 km/h in your [Dacia Sandero Stepway](/vehicles/dacia-sandero-stepway), you will see an 80 km/h sign, followed just 150 meters later by a 60 km/h sign marking a small rural intersection. \n\nMost European drivers simply lift their foot off the accelerator, coasting down from 100 km/h to 78 km/h as they pass the 60 km/h sign. The Gendarmerie laser operator is positioned 100 meters past that 60 km/h sign. At 78 km/h (retained speed 72 km/h), you are clocked at 12 km/h over the limit. In Morocco, speed limit signs are **point-of-sign absolute**: you must complete your braking *before* the nose of your car crosses the vertical plane of the 60 km/h sign.",
