                "content": "When a Gendarme or police officer steps into the road and motions toward the shoulder with a whistle or red baton, how you handle the next three minutes dictates whether you leave with a clean legal transaction or become a target for informal solicitation.\n\n### Step 1: The Approach & Decorum\nSignal immediately, pull completely off the asphalt onto the gravel shoulder, turn on your hazard lights, roll down your window, and **stay inside the vehicle** unless asked to step out to inspect the radar screen. Keep your hands visible on the wheel. Greet the officer respectfully (*\"Salam Alaikum, mon Commandant\"* or *\"Bonjour Monsieur l'agent\"*). Never adopt an aggressive, entitled tone; Moroccan officers hold wide procedural discretion regarding vehicle safety inspections.\n\n### Step 2: Document Presentation\nHave your physical documents stored in the glovebox or door pocket—never in the trunk. Hand over:\n1. Your original national driving license.\n2. Your passport (so they can verify your tourist entry date).\n3. The ZwinCar vehicle folder containing the *Carte Grise* (registration), valid Insurance Certificate, and Rental Contract (*Contrat de Location*).\n\n### Step 3: Verification of the Offense\nThe officer will state your speed (e.g., *\"Vous rouliez à 82 au lieu de 60\"*). If it was a mobile radar check, calmly ask: *\"Puis-je voir la photo sur la jumelle, s'il vous plaît ?\"* (May I see the photo on the binoculars, please?). The officer will either show you the handheld unit or call his colleague via radio to confirm your exact plate number and timestamp.\n\n### Step 4: Payment & The Non-Negotiable 'Quittance'\nIf the infraction is valid, the officer will ask if you wish to pay the *Amende Transactionnelle et Forfaitaire* immediately on the spot or have your license retained against a temporary permit to pay later at a perception office. **Always opt to pay on the spot in cash (Dirhams)** to secure the 50% discount and keep your physical driving license.\n\nHere is the golden rule of driving in Morocco: **Never hand over cash without receiving an official government receipt (*une quittance*).**\n* Every legitimate traffic enforcement unit carries a serialized, multi-carbon-copy receipt book issued by the Ministry of Equipment and Finance.\n* The receipt is a formal document (*Procès-Verbal & Quittance de paiement*) featuring the Kingdom of Morocco crest, a unique serial number, your name, vehicle plate, the exact article of the law violated, and **an adhesive fiscal stamp (*timbre fiscal*) or ink stamp marking the exact Dirham value paid (150 MAD, 300 MAD, or 400 MAD)**.\n* If an officer hints at a \"special tourist price\" of 100 or 200 MAD *\"sans papier\"* (without paperwork), decline firmly and politely: *\"Monsieur l'agent, c'est une voiture de location ZwinCar. Mon contrat m'oblige à présenter la quittance officielle à l'agence, sinon je suis pénalisé. Je préfère payer le tarif légal avec la quittance.\"* (Officer, this is a rental car. My contract requires me to present the official receipt to the agency; I prefer to pay the legal rate with the receipt).\n\nNo officer will ever argue with a driver who insists on paying the full legal discounted fine directly into the state treasury against an official receipt.",
