                "id": "adm-architecture-closed-vs-open-tolls",
                "heading": "The ADM Network Architecture: Closed Ticket Systems vs. Open Barrier Traps",
                "content": "Operated under Royal Concession by the *Société Nationale des Autoroutes du Maroc* (ADM) and governed by Law 4-89 on highway infrastructure, Morocco’s 1,800-kilometer expressway network is engineered to European *péage* standards. However, foreign drivers routinely incur statutory fines because they fail to distinguish between the two distinct toll collection architectures operating simultaneously across the country: the **Système Fermé** (Closed System) and the **Système Ouvert** (Open System).\n\n### 1. The Closed System (Système Fermé)\nDeployed on long-haul intercity arteries—such as the A3 south of Berrechid to Agadir, the A2 from Rabat to Oujda, and the A5 south of Kenitra—the closed system calculates your tariff based on exact distance traveled. \n* **Entry Procedure:** When passing an entry plaza (*Gare d'entrée*), you pull a thermal magnetic ticket from an automated pillar by pressing the illuminated green button.\n* **Exit Procedure:** At your destination exit plaza (*Gare de sortie*), you hand the ticket to a booth attendant or insert it into an automated terminal, which reads the magnetic strip and displays the exact Dirham amount.\n* **The Lost Ticket Penalty (*Perte de Ticket*):** Under ADM general tariff regulations, losing, creasing, or demagnetizing your entry ticket triggers an immediate, non-negotiable punitive charge. You will be billed for the **maximum possible distance** on that contiguous highway network from its farthest origin point. For example, losing your ticket between Settat and Marrakech (normally 52 MAD) forces the booth system to charge you from the furthest northern closed-system node, costing upwards of 180 MAD (€16.50).\n\n### 2. The Open System (Système Ouvert)\nUsed on high-density commuter corridors—specifically the A1 between Casablanca and Rabat, the Tit Mellil bypass, and the Casablanca–Berrechid trunk—the open system eliminates entry tickets entirely. You simply encounter mainline full-width toll plazas (*Gares de péage pleine voie*) where every Class 1 vehicle pays a flat, fixed tariff (e.g., 23 MAD at the Bouznika plaza between Casablanca and Rabat). \n\nThe operational trap occurs at **transition nodes**, such as the Sidi Allal El Bahraoui plaza east of Rabat or the Berrechid plaza south of Casablanca. Here, drivers transition directly from an open flat-rate zone into a closed ticketed corridor. Inattentive tourists frequently mistake the ticket dispenser barrier for an open Jawaz lane, tailgating the car ahead without pulling a ticket, which triggers a Class 3 traffic violation and maximum fare assessment at the final exit.",
