                "heading": "Highway Breakdowns, Fuel Monopolies & the 5050 Emergency Protocol",
                "content": "Once you pass through an ADM entry barrier, you enter a privatized legal jurisdiction where standard municipal road rules regarding towing and mechanical assistance no longer apply. Understanding ADM's operational monopoly is vital if you suffer a tire blowout, mechanical failure, or fuel exhaustion.\n\n### The Private Tow Truck Ban & The '5050' Call Center\nPrivate tow trucks (*Dépannage privé*) and even your rental agency’s third-party insurance contractors are **strictly prohibited by law from entering the ADM highway network** to rescue a stranded vehicle. Only official yellow-and-orange ADM patrol and recovery vehicles hold legal jurisdiction to operate on the carriageway.\n* **Step 1:** Pull as far right onto the emergency hard shoulder (*Bande d'Arrêt d'Urgence* - BAU) as possible, turn your wheels away from traffic, activate hazard lights, and place your reflective warning triangle 30 to 50 meters behind the car.\n* **Step 2:** Have all passengers exit via the right-hand doors and stand **behind the metal Armco safety barrier**.\n* **Step 3:** Dial **5050** from any mobile phone (works with foreign SIMs; standard call rates apply) or walk to the nearest orange SOS pillar (*Borne d'appel d'urgence*), positioned every 2.0 kilometers along the entire network.\n* **Step 4:** Official ADM towing rates are regulated by ministerial decree. Towing a Class 1 passenger car from the breakdown site to the nearest highway exit plaza costs a flat, standardized fee (typically between **150 MAD and 300 MAD** depending on time of day). Only once the ADM flatbed drops your rental car outside the toll barrier can your rental company's private mechanic or replacement vehicle take over.\n\n### Service Area Intervals & Fuel Grade Audits\nADM service areas (*Aires de repos et de service*) are spaced roughly every 40 to 60 kilometers on major trunks (A1, A2, A3, A5), featuring major national fuel distributors (Afriquia, Shell, TotalEnergies, Winxo, Ziz, Ola Energy). However, two critical exceptions exist:\n1. **The Atlas Crossing (Marrakech to Agadir - A3):** Between the Chichaoua service area and the Imintanoute mountains, steep gradients increase fuel consumption by up to 40%. Never enter the Amskroud mountain descent or ascent with less than one-third of a tank.\n2. **The Eastern Corridor (Fes to Oujda - A2):** Between Taza and Guercif/Taourirt, service areas are sparse and occasionally experience temporary fuel stockouts during peak summer weeks.\n\n### Diesel vs. Sans Plomb Naming Forensics\nOver 80% of Moroccan rental fleets—including the Renault Clio 5 and Hyundai Tucson—run on high-pressure common-rail Diesel engines. At highway pumps:\n* **Gasoil** (or *Diesel 10 ppm* / *V-Power Gasoil* / *Excellium Diesel*) = **Diesel**. \n* **Sans Plomb (SP95)** = **Unleaded Petrol/Gasoline**.\nUnlike Europe, pump handle colors are **not standardized** across Moroccan brands (a green handle at one station may dispense Diesel, while at another it dispenses Unleaded). Always verify the written word **GASOIL** or **SANS PLOMB** on the pump chassis and match it against the sticker inside your rental car’s fuel filler flap before authorizing the pump attendant.",
