                "id": "nocturnal-checkpoints-gendarmerie-protocol",
                "heading": "Navigating Nocturnal Police & Gendarmerie Checkpoints (*Barrages Judiciaires*)",
                "content": "After nightfall, security enforcement across Morocco shifts into high-alert posture. Both the **DGSN (Sûreté Nationale)** at municipal entrances/exits and the **Gendarmerie Royale** along rural intersections deploy static and mobile nocturnal roadblocks (*barrages de contrôle*).\n\nFor an uninitiated foreign driver, approaching a rural Moroccan checkpoint at 22:00 can be disorienting: portable steel spike strips (*herses*) are laid across the tarmac creating a tight chicane, illuminated only by weak battery-powered flashing amber beacons or a single handheld red/green LED wand.\n\n### The 5-Step Forensic Protocol for Night Checkpoints\nFailure to execute the exact visual and speed protocol at a night *barrage* can trigger an immediate pursuit or an **Article 184 Class 1 violation** (refusal to comply with an order to stop / *refus d'obtempérer*), which carries a **700 to 1,400 MAD fine** and immediate license confiscation.\n\n1. **Deceleration Phase (200m Out):** The moment you spot a round white sign reading **\"HALTE GENDARMERIE\"** or **\"STOP POLICE\"**—or see flashing blue/amber lights—drop speed progressively from 80 km/h down to **20 km/h** before reaching the first cone.\n2. **Headlight Etiquette (50m Out):** Immediately switch from high beams (*feux de route*) and even low beams (*feux de croisement*) to your **parking/position lights (*feux de position / veilleuses*)** if traffic conditions allow, or keep low beams strictly dipped. Blinding an armed Gendarme standing in the roadway with LED matrix headlights is the quickest way to guarantee a 30-minute vehicle strip-search.\n3. **Cabin Illumination (The Golden Rule):** Turn **ON your interior cabin dome light (*plafonnier*)** and roll your driver-side window completely down before entering the spike-strip chicane. Officers at night are scanning for contraband, impaired drivers, and occupancy compliance. Illuminating the interior voluntarily signals local fluency and immediate transparency.\n4. **The Stop vs. Slow-Roll:** If there is a physical **STOP / قف** sign placed in the middle of the lane, come to a **100% mechanical standstill** (0 km/h on the digital cluster) even if the officer is standing 10 meters away drinking tea. Wait until the officer makes a distinct forward sweeping motion with their flashlight or hand before releasing the clutch/brake.\n5. **Document Readiness:** Keep your physical driver's license, passport, rental agreement (*contrat de location*), and the vehicle's grey card copy (*carte grise*) + insurance certificate (*attestation d'assurance*) clipped together in the center console—never in the trunk.\n\n### Nocturnal Infrared Speed Enforcement\nDo not assume that radar guns sleep when the sun goes down. Since 2024, the Gendarmerie Royale has deployed tripod-mounted **TruCAM II laser-infrared speed cameras** with night-vision optical sensors along the N1, N2, N8, and N9 approaches to major towns (where speed limits drop abruptly from 100 km/h to 80 km/h, and then **60 km/h** inside dark perimeter zones). Exceeding the limit by 20 to 30 km/h triggers an on-the-spot **300 MAD (€28)** fine; 30 to 50 km/h over costs **500 MAD (€46)**.",
