                "id": "tactical-mitigation-emergency-protocol",
                "heading": "Tactical Mitigation & Emergency Protocol: What to Do If Caught Out After Dark",
                "content": "Despite disciplined logistics, mechanical delays, slow border crossings at Tangier Med, or lingering too long at Ait Benhaddou can force you onto a rural Moroccan road after sunset. If you must execute a nocturnal transit on an N or R route, switch immediately to defensive survival protocols.\n\n### 1. The 'Le Lièvre' (Pace Rabbit) Technique\nNever lead the pack on an unfamiliar Moroccan rural road at night. Instead, identify a modern **CTM or Supratours intercity coach**, or a fast-moving local SUV (look for Casablanca `-A-` or Marrakech `-26-` license plates) driving at a sensible pace (75–85 km/h).\n* **Tactical Positioning:** Slot in **100 to 150 meters behind them** (a 4-to-5-second following gap).\n* **Why It Works:** Professional CTM drivers drive these exact corridors every night and know the location of every subsided culvert, unmarked speed bump, and radar trap. Their high-output roofline lights illuminate pedestrians and donkey carts 200 meters ahead of you, and if an unlit obstacle or stray animal enters the lane, their brake lights and suspension pitch give you a massive 4-second early warning buffer.\n\n### 2. Pre-Flight Optical & Mechanical Audit\nBefore departing your afternoon stop, perform a 90-second inspection on your rental vehicle:\n* **Headlight Leveling Dial:** Locate the manual thumbwheel dial to the left of the steering column (standard on Dacia Duster, Hyundai Tucson, and Renault models). If your trunk is loaded with four suitcases, the rear suspension squats, pointing your low beams into the trees and blinding oncoming trucks. Set the dial from `0` to `1` or `2` to aim the beam back onto the asphalt.\n* **Windshield Degreasing:** Moroccan diesel soot mixed with Saharan red dust creates a micro-film on the glass. When hit by oncoming high beams at night, this film refracts light across the entire windshield, turning it opaque. Clean both the exterior AND interior glass with alcohol wipes before dusk, and verify the washer fluid reservoir is full.\n\n### 3. Emergency Breakdown & Collision Numbers\nIf you suffer a tire blowout on a sharp stone shoulder or strike an animal after dark, **do not exit the vehicle into the active roadway without a reflective vest** (mandatory in all rental cars under Law 52-05; fine for absence is 300 MAD). Move all passengers behind the roadside rock wall or well off the shoulder before placing your warning triangle 100 meters back.\n\n| Emergency Service | Direct Dial (Moroccan SIM / Roaming) | Jurisdiction & Use Case |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Gendarmerie Royale** | **177** | All rural roads (N, R, P) and Autoroutes outside city limits |\n| **Police (Sûreté Nationale)** | **19** (Landline) / **112** (Mobile) | Strictly inside urban municipal perimeters |\n| **Ambulance / Protection Civile** | **15** or **150** | Medical emergencies and trauma extraction |\n| **ADM Highway Assistance** | **5050** | 24/7 patrol & towing strictly on toll motorways (A1–A8) |",
