            {
                "id": "strategic-refueling-corridors-and-airport-dropoffs",
                "heading": "Strategic Fuel Corridors, Jerrycan Laws & Airport Return Audits",
                "content": "While coastal Morocco has dense station coverage every 3 to 5 kilometers, desert and high-mountain transits require aerospace-grade fuel management. Running out of fuel (*panne sèche*) on a Moroccan toll highway is treated as an infraction by the *Gendarmerie Royale* if it creates a safety hazard, and towing fees from monopolized highway recovery trucks start at **600 MAD (€55)**.\n\n### Critical Low-Density Fuel Corridors\nNever drop below a **half-tank (50% gauge)** when entering the following sectors:\n* **Marrakech to Ouarzazate via Tizi n'Tichka (N9):** Refuel at the Afriquia or TotalEnergies station in **Aït Ourir** or **Taddert** before climbing the pass. Prolonged 2nd and 3rd gear climbs at 2,260m altitude spike fuel consumption by 40%.\n* **Agadir to Marrakech Highway (A7 across High Atlas):** There is a notorious **92-kilometer gap** with steep elevation gains between the Chichaoua rest stop and the Amezmiz/Agadir mountain exits. Ensure you have at least 150 km of range before passing Imintanoute.\n* **Foum Zguid to Zagora / Tata (N12/R108):** Stations in rural oasis towns occasionally run completely dry of Gasoil for 24 hours waiting for the coastal tanker delivery. Always top off in Ouarzazate, Agdz, or Taroudant before pushing into the deep south.\n\n### The Legal Ban on Loose Fuel & Jerrycans\nUnder Moroccan civil protection and anti-smuggling security directives, gas stations are strictly prohibited from dispensing petrol or diesel into plastic water bottles, jugs, or uncertified containers (*vente d'essence en vrac*). If you attempt to buy 5 liters of fuel in a Sidi Ali water bottle as an emergency reserve, the station manager will refuse—and in urban centers, they are legally required to require a police permit (*autorisation de police*) even for standard metal jerrycans to prevent arson and unlicensed roadside reselling.\n\n### The Airport Rental Return Audit\nMoroccan car rental agencies operate on a strict **Same-to-Same (Niveau à Niveau)** fuel policy rather than a universal Full-to-Full mandate. If you receive your vehicle at 3/8 (three bars on a digital Dacia gauge), you must return it at 3/8. Returning it with less triggers a penalty surcharge of **15 to 18 MAD per liter** plus an administrative refueling fee.\n* **Marrakech Menara (RAK):** Top off at the **TotalEnergies or Afriquia on Avenue Guemassa** (2.2 km from the terminal).\n* **Casablanca Mohammed V (CMN):** Refuel at the **Shell or Afriquia station right before the Nouaceur Airport toll plaza**.\n* **Agadir Al Massira (AGA):** Refuel at the **Afriquia station in Ait Melloul or Temsia** along the N10 highway (4 km prior to the airport roundabout).",
                "callout": {
                    "type": "warning",
                    "text": "During vehicle pickup, take a clear timestamped smartphone photo of the dashboard showing both the odometer mileage and the exact digital fuel bars before leaving the airport parking lot. Digital gauges on Renault/Dacia fleets drop abruptly after 40 km if the previous renter only 'micro-topped' the filler neck."
                }
            }
