                "id": "fuel-stops-1000-kasbahs",
                "heading": "Fuel Logistics & Gas Station Audit on the Route of 1000 Kasbahs",
                "content": "Running low on fuel or ingesting contaminated diesel from an aging rural tank will immediately derail your itinerary. While the main N9 and N10 arteries are well-serviced, stretches of the southern N12 between Rissani and Tansikht feature long gaps where small village stations periodically run dry of **Gasoil (Diesel)** or **Sans Plomb (Unleaded)**. Modern European rental fleets in Morocco utilize sensitive high-pressure common-rail diesel injectors (dCi and D-4D engines) that require clean **50 ppm or 10 ppm ultra-low-sulfur diesel**.\n\nWhen executing your **route 1000 kasbahs gas stations** strategy, strictly patronize Tier-1 national and international networks: **Afriquia, Shell, TotalEnergies, Winxo, and Petrom**. Avoid unbranded independent pumps in isolated desert hamlets unless facing an absolute emergency.\n\n### Strategic Fueling Checkpoints (Marrakech to Merzouga)\n1. **Aït Ourir (N9 - Km 35 from Marrakech):** Last dense cluster of Shell and Afriquia stations before climbing the High Atlas. Top up here so you aren't climbing the 2,260m Tichka gradient on a quarter tank.\n2. **Ouarzazate Entrance & Exit (N9/N10):** Major logistical hub. The **TotalEnergies** at the western roundabout and the **Afriquia** on the eastern exit toward Skoura have high fuel turnover, pristine clean tanks, modern restrooms, and accept international Visa/Mastercard.\n3. **Boumalne Dades & Tinghir (N10):** Both towns have reliable Shell and Petrom stations at their eastern and western entry points. Current fuel prices in the south carry a slight inland transport surcharge over Casablanca/Marrakech: expect **13.10 to 13.60 MAD/L (€1.20–€1.25)** for Diesel and **14.90 to 15.40 MAD/L (€1.38–€1.42)** for Unleaded.\n4. **Erfoud & Rissani (N13/N12):** The final major urban fueling hubs before the Erg Chebbi dunes. Fill your tank to 100% at the **Shell station in Erfoud** or the **Afriquia/Petrom stations at the entrance of Rissani**.\n\n| Corridor Segment | Distance | Station Density | Credit Card Acceptance | Forensic Recommendation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Marrakech to Ouarzazate (N9)** | 195 km | Moderate (Taddart, Aguelmous) | 70% (Network drops in mountains) | Fill in Marrakech; carry 300 MAD cash backup. |\n| **Ouarzazate to Tinghir (N10)** | 168 km | High (Skoura, M'Gouna, Dades) | 85% at Shell/Total/Afriquia | Top up in Ouarzazate or Boumalne Dades. |\n| **Tinghir to Merzouga (N10/N13)** | 200 km | Moderate (Tinjdad, Erfoud, Rissani) | 50% (Cash preferred east of Tinjdad) | Mandatory full tank top-up in Erfoud or Rissani. |\n| **Rissani to Nkob (Southern N12)** | 220 km | Low (Alnif, Tazzarine only) | <20% (Almost strictly Cash) | Never enter the N12 at Rissani with less than 60% tank. |\n\n**Tire Pressure & Mechanical Check:** Every major Afriquia and TotalEnergies station features a dedicated *lavage/graissage* bay with an air compressor. Before leaving Ouarzazate, ask the attendant to verify your tire pressures match the driver-side door jamb sticker (typically **2.2 to 2.4 bar**). Give the attendant a **5 to 10 MAD (€0.50–€0.90)** tip. Do not under-inflate your tires for paved desert roads; under-inflation on hot Saharan tarmac causes sidewall overheating and catastrophic blowouts.",
