                "id": "radar-traps-gendarmes-checkpoints",
                "heading": "Speed Traps, Gendarmerie Checkpoints & Moroccan Traffic Law",
                "content": "The N9, N10, and N12 corridors are heavily patrolled by the **Gendarmerie Royale** (rural highway police dressed in grey uniforms) and the **Sûreté Nationale (DGSN)** inside urban perimeters like Ouarzazate, Tinghir, and Erfoud. Because the desert roads east of Boumalne Dades feature long, flat, straight stretches of high-visibility asphalt, tourists routinely fall into speed transition traps.\n\nUnder the Moroccan Highway Code (**Loi n° 52-05 portant code de la route, updated by Loi 116-14**), speed enforcement operates with a strict 10% maximum tolerance on automated radar guns (TruCAM LTI 20/20 laser units with high-definition optical cameras are used throughout the Drâa-Tafilalet region).\n\n### The Classic N10 Transition Trap\nAs you approach every small oasis village along the Route of the 1000 Kasbahs (such as Imider, Taghzout, or Khorbat), the speed limit drops precipitously in steps over 300 meters: **100 km/h → 80 km/h → 60 km/h**, and occasionally **40 km/h** near rural school zones. Officers stand concealed behind palm trunks or mudbrick walls 100 meters *past* the 60 km/h sign, or 200 meters *before* the end-of-limit sign as you accelerate out of town.\n\n### Fine Structure & Legal Payment Protocol\nIf clocked exceeding the limit by less than 20 km/h (e.g., driving 68 km/h in a 60 km/h zone after tolerance adjustment), it is classified as a **3rd Class Contravention**:\n* **Standard Fine:** 300 MAD.\n* **Immediate Spot-Payment Discount (*Amende Transactionnelle et Forfaitaire*):** Under Article 219 of the Highway Code, if you pay immediately in cash on the spot, the fine is legally reduced by 50% to **150 MAD (€14)**.\n*Exceeding the limit by 20 to 30 km/h is a **2nd Class Contravention** (500 MAD base, reduced to **300 MAD / €28** if paid immediately).*\n\nIf you are stopped, follow this exact forensic protocol:\n1. Pull over safely onto the gravel shoulder, switch on your hazard lights, and roll down your window.\n2. Hand over the three mandatory pouches/documents: your physical **Driver's License**, your **Passport** (to prove entry stamp validity under 90 days), and the rental vehicle's grey vinyl folder containing the **Carte Grise (registration), Assurance (insurance), and Autorisation de Circulation (rental contract)**.\n3. If fined, calmly request the official receipt (*quittance*). Every legitimate 150 MAD or 300 MAD payment requires the officer to tear a numbered carbon receipt from an official government booklet. Never hand over loose cash without receiving a *quittance*.\n\n### Fixed Checkpoint (*Barrage Judiciaire*) Etiquette\nAt the entrances and exits of Ouarzazate, Tinghir, Erfoud, and Rissani, you will encounter permanent police checkpoints marked by chicane barriers and a red hexagonal sign reading **HALTE POLICE / قف الشرطة** or **GENDARMERIE ROYALE**.\n* **Rule 1:** Even if the barrier is open and no officer is standing in the lane, you **must slow down to a crawl (10 km/h)** and come to a complete dead stop at the *Halte* sign.\n* **Rule 2:** At night, turn off your main headlights (keep parking lights on so you don't blind the officers) and **switch on your interior cabin dome light** so the officers can clearly see the occupants.\n* **Rule 3:** Wait until the officer makes a clear forward hand-wave gesture before accelerating. Foreign tourists in rental cars are waved through 95% of the time in under 3 seconds if they respect the dead-stop rule.",
