                "heading": "2. Surf Racks, Roof Damage & Vehicle Deposit Forensics",
                "content": "Strapping surfboards to rental cars is the single largest cause of unfair or disputed deposit deductions along the Atlantic coastline. Under Moroccan Road Traffic Law (*Loi 52-05*, Article 97), unsecured exterior cargo is subject to an immediate 300 MAD fine, but the financial threat from rental agencies dwarfs the police penalty.\n\n### Soft Racks vs. Factory Roof Rails\nBudget travelers frequently bring universal soft racks (foam pads with nylon webbing routed through the interior cabin). When used incorrectly on vehicles like the Renault Clio or Dacia Sandero without factory roof rails, these cause three severe structural issues:\n\n1. **Weatherstrip Crushing:** Straps pulled through the door frame squash the rubber weatherstrips (*joints d'étanchéité*). Driving at 100 km/h on the R207 causes high-frequency strap vibration, which slices into the rubber or permanently deforms the seal, leading to interior water leaks during rain or coastal fog. Replacement costs range from 800 to 1,500 MAD per door at franchise body shops.\n2. **Roof Metal Flexing:** Placing three 8-foot foam boards directly over a bare car roof without crossbars creates downward aerodynamic pressure at speed. The thin sheet metal of modern B-segment vehicles dents inward easily. Agencies classify this as non-collision structural damage, deducting up to 3,000 MAD from your pre-authorization hold.\n3. **Salt-and-Sand Paint Abrasion:** Fine Atlantic sand trapped beneath soft pads acts as 80-grit sandpaper against the clear coat under heavy wind buffeting. Even minor scuffs on the roof clearcoat trigger automated paint penalty clauses (1,200–2,000 MAD per panel).\n\nIf you travel with boards, specify a vehicle factory-fitted with integrated longitudinal roof rails, such as the Dacia Sandero Stepway or Dacia Duster. Bring your own aero bar straps and lockable tie-downs. Alternatively, travel with single-board day bags placed completely inside the vehicle with the rear bench dropped down 60/40.",
