                "content": "The Marrakech-Essaouira-Agadir corridor is among the most heavily monetized traffic enforcement corridors in southern Morocco. Surveillance relies on three distinct layers: permanent digital Vitronic radar towers, mobile laser units operated by the *Gendarmerie Royale*, and manual city entry roundabouts manned by the *Sûreté Nationale*.\n\n### Fixed Vitronic Multi-Lane Towers\nThese tall, grey cylindrical radar poles calculate both approaching and receding vehicle speeds simultaneously across multiple lanes, taking daytime and infrared night captures. Key locations to mark on your navigation:\n- **R207 Km 42 (near Loudaya):** Enforces a strict 100 km/h drop from the highway exit.\n- **R207 Km 112 (Chichaoua Bypass):** Placed exactly where long, flat straights tempt drivers above 120 km/h.\n- **R207 Km 162 (Ounagha Junction):** Drops from 100 km/h down to 80 km/h, then 60 km/h in a 300-meter span. Radars are calibrated to snap drivers who coast rather than brake firmly.\n- **N1 Km 34 North of Agadir (near Taghazout Bay exit):** 80 km/h strictly enforced before the dual-carriageway expands.\n\n### Mobile Handheld Laser Protocol\nGendarmes position themselves with tripod-mounted Truvelo or ProLaser 4 systems concealed behind argan trees on descents. Common hot zones include the long straightaway after Smimou and the Tamri banana corridor.\n\nIf flagged down by a Gendarme standing in the roadway holding a circular red-and-white sign:\n1. Indicate right, come to a complete stop 10 meters before the officer, turn off your high beams, and keep your hands on the steering wheel.\n2. Do not exit the car until requested.\n3. Under *Loi 52-05*, Article 184 (minor infractions), exceeding the limit by less than 20 km/h incurs a 300 MAD on-the-spot fine. Exceeding by 20–30 km/h jumps to 500 MAD; over 30 km/h is 700 MAD.\n4. Always request the official carbon-copy receipt (*Quittance de paiement*). Cash is required immediately unless you possess a Moroccan driver’s license, in which case license retention protocols apply.",
