                "content": "Driving the Atlantic seaboard introduces dynamic environmental hazards unfamiliar to standard highway drivers. The microclimates between the semi-arid Haouz plain of Marrakech and the maritime fringe of the Souss-Massa demand defensive car control.\n\n### The Alizé Crosswinds (Essaouira Corridor)\nThe trade winds accelerating off the Atlantic funnel through coastal valleys, regularly reaching gusts of 60 to 80 km/h between May and October. When driving a high-profile vehicle like a Dacia Sandero Stepway or Dacia Duster, you will experience sudden lateral displacement when exiting windbreaks, tree lines, or road cuttings onto open bridges.\n- Maintain two hands firmly on the wheel at the 9-and-3 position.\n- Reduce cruising speed from 100 km/h to 80 km/h during high-wind advisories.\n- Keep a three-second following distance from intercity CTM buses and overloaded semi-trailers, which drift across center lines when hit by gusts.\n\n### Coastal Mist (Brume de Mer) and Salt Encrustation\nBetween Taghazout and Tamri, late-afternoon maritime inversions drag dense marine fog across the asphalt in minutes, plummeting visibility from 5 km to under 25 meters. Salt spray combines with road dust to create a microscopic, greasy emulsion on the windshield. If your rental car’s washer fluid reservoir is empty (a common oversight among budget providers), wiper blades will smear this salt film into an opaque grey sheet that renders driving into the setting sun impossible. Test the wiper jets before leaving Marrakech.\n\n### Livestock and Roadside Tourist Scams\nIn the argan groves between Ounagha and Smimou, you will observe goats standing in the lower branches of argan trees. Exercise extreme skepticism: handlers frequently tether these animals artificially to extort foreign motorists who stop for photos. When vehicles park abruptly on the soft unpaved shoulder, they risk dropping a passenger-side wheel into hidden irrigation ditches or running over sharp scrub roots that puncture low-profile street tires.",
