                "content": "Selecting the appropriate rental vehicle for Dades and Todra depends heavily on your planned route limits and passenger loading.\n\n```\nVEHICLE FLEET CAPABILITY AUDIT: GORGE TRANSIT\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPlatform Category     | Ground Clearance | Powertrain       | Gorge Floor | High Pass\n                      | (Minimum)        | Suitability      | (Paved)     | (M'Semrir-Agoudal)\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDacia Sandero Stepway | 174 mm           | 1.0 TCe (Turbo)  | EXCELLENT   | FORBIDDEN\nDacia Duster 4x4      | 210 mm           | 1.5 dCi (Diesel) | OPTIMAL     | COMPLIANT\nToyota Prado TX 4x4   | 215 mm           | 2.8 D-4D (Low-R) | SURPLUS     | OPTIMAL\nStandard Hatchback    | 120-135 mm       | 1.2L Nat. Asp.   | MARGINAL    | DESTROYED\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n```\n\n### Powertrain Architecture: Why Forced Induction Matters\nAvoid small naturally aspirated petrol engines when traveling with a full passenger load. Ascending the long approach from Ouarzazate through the Valley of the Roses (Kelaat M'Gouna) into the gorges involves high sustained engine loads against strong prevailing winds. Turbocharged diesel options (such as the Renault-Nissan 1.5 dCi found in the Duster) or turbocharged petrol platforms provide low-end torque availability starting from 1,750 RPM, preventing continuous redlining and overheating on steep grades.\n\n### Ground Clearance Considerations\nEven on the paved sectors of the R703 and R704, minor rockfalls (*chutes de pierres*) frequently deposit fist-sized scree across the roadway. A vehicle with less than 150 mm of ground clearance risks severe impact damage to the oil pan, transmission bell housing, or exhaust catalytic converter when forced onto shoulders to bypass oncoming tour coaches.",
