                "id": "five-routes-requiring-true-4wd",
                "heading": "The 5% Exception: Moroccan Routes Where a True 4x4 is Mandatory",
                "content": "While high-clearance 2WD crossovers handle the vast majority of tourist itineraries, Morocco possesses severe alpine and Saharan corridors where attempting passage without a genuine 4x4 transfer case is mechanically destructive or life-threatening. Here is the forensic status of Morocco’s most famous rough routes as of 2026:\n\n### 1. Erg Chegaga via Lake Iriqui (M'Hamid to Foum Zguid)\nUnlike Merzouga (Erg Chebbi), where the asphalt goes right to the dunes, reaching the wild dunes of **Erg Chegaga** requires traversing 60 km to 90 km of deep *fesh-fesh* (talcum-powder silt), soft sand dunes, and the baked mud flats of Lake Iriqui. **Requirement:** Heavy-duty 4x4 with Low Range (Toyota Prado TX or Mitsubishi Pajero) and deflated tires (1.2 to 1.5 bar). A 2WD Duster will bury itself up to its axles within 15 minutes of passing M'Hamid el Ghizlane.\n\n### 2. The Jbel Saghro Crossing (Tizi n'Tazazert Pass)\nConnecting Nekob to Boumalne Dades across the volcanic anti-Atlas mountains, the Tizi n'Tazazert pass sits at 2,283 meters. While northern sections near Tagdilt have seen sporadic grading, the southern switchbacks remain unsealed, narrow, and littered with sharp volcanic basalt. **Requirement:** minimum **Dacia Duster 4x4** (using 4WD Lock on steep hairpins) or **Toyota Prado**.\n\n### 3. The Dades-Todra Gorge High Link (Agoudal / Msemrir Loop - R704)\nFor years, the R704 connecting the upper Dades Valley (Msemrir) to the Todra Valley (Tamtattouchte) via the 2,700-meter Tizi n'Ouano pass was a notorious 4x4-only track. Recently, paving crews sealed the majority of this corridor. **However**, winter snowmelt and autumn flash floods routinely wash out 200-meter sections of asphalt, leaving steep, muddy river crossings and loose shale bypasses. **Requirement:** In dry summer months, a **high-clearance 2WD Duster** can pass with extreme caution; between October and May, a **4x4** is mandatory.\n\n### 4. Cirque de Jaffar (Midelt to Imilchil)\nThe classic lower gorge route of the Cirque de Jaffar is impassable to standard vehicles and requires rock-crawling capability. Even the newer higher bypass track features severe washouts, deep clay ruts after rain, and steep gradients. **Requirement:** True 4x4 with high articulation.\n\n### 5. High Atlas Winter Snow (Ifrane, Michlifen, Oukaimeden)\nBetween December and March, temperatures in the Middle and High Atlas drop to -10°C. Moroccan rental cars are fitted exclusively with summer tires (*pneus été*). When snow blankets the N8 around Ifrane or the climb to Oukaimeden, the Ministry of Equipment sets up *Gendarmerie Royale* checkpoints (*barrages de neige*) that physically block 2WD vehicles lacking snow chains.",
