                "id": "piste-survival-protocol-2wd",
                "heading": "Mechanical Survival Guide: How to Drive High-Clearance 2WDs on Pistes",
                "content": "When you book a high-clearance crossover like a **Dacia Duster** or **Dacia Sandero Stepway** and need to navigate a 3 km unpaved hotel access track in Agafay, Skoura, or Ouarzazate, your driving technique determines whether you arrive intact or on the back of a flatbed truck.\n\n### Step 1: The Tire Pressure Protocol\nRental agencies in Marrakech and Agadir chronically over-inflate tires to **2.5 or 2.8 bar (36–40 PSI)** to reduce rolling resistance and tread wear on highways. When a tire inflated to 2.8 bar hits a sharp limestone rock on a piste, the rubber has zero flex; it pops like a balloon.\n* If you are only driving a short 2 km hotel track, leave the pressure alone and drive at walking pace (**10–15 km/h**).\n* If you are driving a longer 15 km gravel road (such as the Oasis de Fint access near Ouarzazate), drop your tire pressure slightly to **2.0 bar (29 PSI)** so the tire tread absorbs sharp stones. **Crucial rule:** You must re-inflate back to 2.3 bar at the very next *Afriquia*, *Shell*, or *TotalEnergies* station (tip the attendant 5 to 10 MAD). Driving at 100 km/h on hot asphalt with under-inflated tires will cause catastrophic sidewall heat failure.\n\n### Step 2: Straddling the Ruts (*Chevaucher l'Ornière*)\nNever drive with your wheels inside the deep tracks left by heavy trucks. Those tracks lower your car’s belly by 10 to 15 cm, ensuring the raised center ridge of rocks (*le bombé central*) smashes directly into your oil pan. Always position your left or right tires directly on top of the center ridge, keeping the lowest point of your engine bay suspended over the deeper rut.\n\n### Step 3: Defeating Washboard Corrugations (*Tôle Ondulée*)\nAgafay tracks are famous for violent rhythmic ripples. Tourists often read off-road myths claiming you should 'speed up to 60 km/h to float over the bumps.' **Do not do this in a 2WD crossover.** At 60 km/h on washboard gravel, a front-wheel-drive car loses steering contact with the ground, the ABS braking system goes completely blind (increasing stopping distances by 300%), and the standard hydraulic shock absorbers will overheat and blow their seals within 10 kilometers. Creep over washboard sections in 1st or 2nd gear at **15–20 km/h**.\n\n### Step 4: Inspect the Spare Tire Before Leaving the City\nBefore signing your rental agreement, open the trunk floor (or inspect the under-chassis winch cradle on 4x2 Dusters) to verify that the spare wheel (*roue de secours*), scissor jack (*cric*), and lug wrench (*clé démonte-roue*) are physically present and that the spare holds air.",
