                "heading": "Chassis Geometry and Underbody Realities: The 210 mm vs 201 mm Fallacy",
                "content": "On paper, the marketing brochures suggest parity: a Dacia Sandero Stepway boasts 201 mm of unladen ground clearance, while the Dacia Duster sits at 210 mm (214 mm on 4x4 variants). In Moroccan fleet auditing, relying on unladen clearance is an expensive mistake.\n\nThe Sandero Stepway utilizes a standard B-segment CMF-B platform with lengthened coil springs and a torsion-beam rear axle. When loaded with three passengers, two 23 kg suitcases, and luggage, the Stepway's dynamic rear clearance compresses by 38 mm to 45 mm. Its approach angle of 17.6° and departure angle of 24.3° expose the low-mounted front intercooler and composite oil pan to steep culverts, ubiquitous *dos d'âne* (Morocco's non-standard, razor-sharp asphalt speed bumps), and broken shoulder drop-offs (*accotements dégradés*).\n\n| Mechanical Metric | Dacia Sandero Stepway (3rd Gen) | Dacia Duster II / III (4x2 / 4x4) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Ride Height (Unladen)** | 201 mm | 210 mm (214 mm on 4x4) |\n| **Ride Height (4 Passengers + 80 kg)** | ~158 mm | ~184 mm |\n| **Approach Angle** | 17.6° | 30.0° |\n| **Departure Angle** | 24.3° | 33.0° (4x4) / 34.0° (4x2) |\n| **Breakover / Ramp Angle** | 19.1° | 21.0° |\n| **Tyre Sidewall Profile** | 205/60 R16 (123 mm sidewall) | 215/65 R16 or 215/60 R17 (139 mm / 129 mm) |\n| **Sump Protection** | Thin pressed thermoplastic | Heavy stamped steel plate (standard on rental specs) |\n\nThe Duster relies on the heavy-duty B0/CMF-B extended architecture designed from inception for emerging-market logging tracks. It features an authentic stamped-steel engine skid plate (skis de protection) and an approach angle of 30.0°. Crucially, its tyre specification—typically 215/65 R16 on commercial rental fleets—offers 139 mm of rubber sidewall, dampening sharp limestone edges along the Dadès gorges that routinely rupture the lower-profile 205/60 R16 tyres fitted to the Stepway.",
