                "heading": "Engine Diagnostics on Atlas Passes: 1.0 TCe Petrol vs 1.5 dCi Diesel",
                "content": "The engine under the bonnet determines your safety margin when overtaking overloaded agricultural flatbeds on the switchbacks of the N9 (Col du Tizi n'Tichka, 2,260 m) or the N10 between Taroudant and Ouarzazate.\n\nMost rental Sandero Stepways in Morocco are fitted with the 1.0-litre TCe 90 hp three-cylinder turbocharged petrol unit, paired either with a 6-speed manual or a CVT gearbox. At sea level in Casablanca or Agadir, the TCe pulls adequately. However, at altitudes above 1,800 metres, atmospheric oxygen density drops by roughly 18% to 22%. While the turbocharger compensates partially, low-end torque suffers significantly. Drivers are forced into first and second gears, driving coolant temperatures up during 40°C summer climbs and demanding constant clutch slipping on 12% gradients.\n\nIn contrast, the enterprise-spec Dacia Duster in Morocco remains dominated by the venerable Renault-Nissan 1.5 dCi (K9K) turbodiesel, producing 110 hp or 115 hp and a muscular 260 Nm of torque accessible from just 1,750 RPM. \n\n```\nEngine Torque Comparison on 10% Ascents:\nSandero Stepway 1.0 TCe : [160 Nm @ 2,100–3,750 RPM] — High stress, gear hunting\nDacia Duster 1.5 dCi     : [260 Nm @ 1,750–2,750 RPM] — Effortless tractor pull\n```\n\nThe 1.5 dCi's high torque reserve allows you to execute mountain passes in third gear without dropping off boost. On single-lane desert links where you must occasionally drop two wheels into unpaved, rutted verges to clear oncoming CTM coaches, the Duster's low-end diesel grunt pulls the chassis back onto the tarmac without wheel spin or clutch burn.",
