                "content": "Rental car marketing in Morocco rarely highlights the stark legal line between *Routes Numérotées* (gazetted numbered roads) and *Pistes Non Goudronnées* (unpaved tracks). \n\nUnder standard Moroccan vehicle rental contracts governed by the Dahir of Obligations and Contracts (DOC) and supervised under Law 52-05 (Highway Code), full insurance coverage—including Collision Damage Waiver (CDW) and Super CDW—is universally void the moment four wheels leave an officially designated, paved public road.\n\n```\nStandard Moroccan Rental Exclusion Clause (Extract):\n\"Sont expressément exclus des garanties d'assurance tous dégâts matériels survenus\nsur des pistes non carrossables, lits d'oueds, et zones hors réseau routier classé,\nla réparation intégrale restant à la charge exclusive du locataire.\"\n```\n\nIf you rent a Sandero Stepway and crack an axle on an unpaved track leading to a remote guesthouse in the Dades Valley:\n1. **Towing Fees:** Piste recovery is completely excluded from standard roadside assistance (*Assistance Maroc*). Commercial recovery operators bill private extraction rates starting at 2,500 to 5,000 MAD (€230–€460).\n2. **Deductible Breach:** The pre-authorized credit card guarantee (franchise), generally 5,000 to 7,000 MAD (€460–€645) for a Stepway, will be retained in full.\n3. **Loss of Use (Chômage du véhicule):** The agency can legally invoice you for every day the vehicle sits in a bodyshop awaiting imported Renault parts, calculated at the peak daily rate under standard commercial terms.\n\nWith a Duster rented under an explicitly authorized 'All-Road' contract (available through select regional agencies in Ouarzazate and Marrakech), specific unpaved secondary trails are legally recognized, provided the vehicle is an authentic 4x4 variant with mechanical multi-mode transmission.",
