                "content": "A comprehensive operational analysis spanning a typical 10-day, 2,000-kilometre Moroccan loop reveals significant cost divergences between these two platforms.\n\n### 1. Real-World Fuel Economy\nMoroccan fuel pricing fluctuates with international indices; diesel (*Gasoil 10-PPM*) averages approximately 12.50 to 13.50 MAD/L, while unleaded petrol (*Super Sans Plomb*) averages 14.50 to 15.50 MAD/L. \n* **Sandero Stepway (1.0 TCe Petrol):** Returns an average of 6.8 to 7.4 L/100 km across mountain and mixed terrain. Total fuel cost for 2,000 km: **~2,150 MAD (€197)**.\n* **Dacia Duster (1.5 dCi Diesel):** Returns an exceptional 5.0 to 5.6 L/100 km under sustained heavy load. Total fuel cost for 2,000 km: **~1,400 MAD (€128)**.\n\nThis 750 MAD (€69) diesel efficiency dividend directly offsets the Duster's higher daily rental base fee over long distances.\n\n### 2. Luggage and Ergonomics: The Family Bottleneck\n\n| Dimensional Attribute | Sandero Stepway | Dacia Duster |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Boot Capacity (Seats Up)** | 328 Litres | 445 Litres (4x2) / 411 Litres (4x4) |\n| **Usable Width Between Arches** | 1,020 mm | 1,005 mm |\n| **Rear Legroom (Knee Clearance)** | 145 mm | 170 mm |\n| **Payload Limit** | 435 kg | 505 kg |\n\nA family of four traveling with two large hardshell suitcases (75 cm) and two cabin bags will quickly exceed the Stepway's 328-litre boot capacity. The rigid parcel shelf will fail to close, forcing bags onto the rear passenger bench—a severe hazard on winding mountain routes and an open invitation for vehicle break-ins during intermediate stops in medina-adjacent surface parking lots.",
