                "heading": "Financial Forensics at RAK: CMI Terminal Mechanics, DCC & Deposit Traps",
                "content": "The most lucrative profit center for low-cost broker franchises operating at Marrakech Menara Airport is not the daily rental rate—it is the financial engineering executed at the payment terminal. Understanding how Moroccan banking rails process foreign cards is critical to protecting your travel liquidity.\n\nAll legitimate card transactions in Morocco route through the *Centre Monétique Interbancaire* (CMI) using ISO 8583 messaging protocols. When a rental agent processes your security deposit (*franchise*), two distinct terminal commands exist:\n\n1. **Pre-Authorization (*Pré-autorisation* - Message Type 0100):** Freezes a specific Dirham amount on your credit line for up to 21–30 days without settling the funds.\n2. **Direct Sale / Capture (*Vente Directe* - Message Type 0200):** Immediately debits the funds from your bank account and transfers them to the merchant's Moroccan bank account.\n\n**The Debit Card Bait-and-Switch:**\nMany travelers arrive at RAK with European or UK neobank cards (Revolut, Monzo, Wise, N26) or standard Visa/Mastercard cards that have 'DEBIT' embossed on the plastic. Legacy airport brokers enforce strict underwriting rules: even if your account holds €20,000 in cash, a card coded with a Debit BIN (*Bank Identification Number*) cannot process a multi-week CMI 0100 Pre-Authorization hold. \n\nWhen the CMI terminal rejects the pre-auth on a debit card, the desk agent presents an ultimatum: either forfeit your pre-paid booking voucher completely, or pay a mandatory 'Super CDW / Zero Excess' insurance upgrade costing 250 to 400 MAD (€23 to €37) per day. Over a 10-day rental, this doubles or triples your total expenditure.\n\n**The 'Vente Directe' Double-FX Trap:**\nAlternatively, some low-tier agencies bypass the pre-authorization limit by running your deposit (often 10,000 to 15,000 MAD) as a **Vente Directe (Sale)**. Because the Dirham actually leaves your foreign currency account, your home bank charges a 2.5% to 3% Foreign Transaction Fee on the outgoing €1,400. Fourteen days after you return the car undamaged, the agency issues a refund. Your bank then applies *another* 2.5% to 3% currency spread on the inbound conversion. You lose €70 to €90 in pure banking friction without a single scratch on the vehicle.\n\n| Financial Vector | Legacy Broker Franchise (Desk) | ZwinCar Forensic Protocol (Parking P2) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Card Type Accepted** | Embossed Credit Card ONLY (Strict BIN check) | Credit, Debit (Revolut/Wise), or Cash |\n| **Deposit Mechanism** | 10,000 – 20,000 MAD CMI Block | 0 MAD to low-cap transparent pre-auth |\n| **Terminal Receipt Verification** | Often obscured or bundled with extras | Explicit *Pré-autorisation* slip verified |\n| **Dynamic Currency Conversion (DCC)** | Frequently forced (6.5% hidden markup) | Strictly billed in local MAD (0% markup) |\n\nAlways inspect the paper slip printed by the mobile CMI terminal in Parking P2. The header must explicitly state **PRÉ-AUTORISATION**—never **VENTE**. Furthermore, if the terminal prompts you to choose between paying in your home currency (EUR/GBP/USD) or Moroccan Dirhams (MAD), **always select MAD**. Selecting your home currency triggers Dynamic Currency Conversion (DCC), legally permitting the terminal acquirer to apply a predatory 5.5% to 7.2% exchange rate surcharge.",
