            {
                "id": "cmn-dropoff-logistics-refueling-timing-audit",
                "heading": "CMN Drop-Off Mechanics: Refueling Forensics, Buffer Windows & Closing the Contract",
                "content": "Returning a rental vehicle to Casablanca Mohammed V Airport requires precise timing due to three consecutive logistical bottlenecks: the Bouskoura highway inflow, the Nouaceur perimeter refueling sweep, and the ONDA terminal security architecture.\n\n**1. The 180-Minute Airport Buffer Rule**:\nUnlike European hubs where rental car drop-off connects directly to the check-in concourse via automated people-movers, CMN enforces a strict luggage X-ray and passport screening perimeter just to **enter the Terminal 1 or Terminal 2 building doors**. During the morning European departure wave (05:30–08:30) and the midday Gulf/North American wave (12:00–15:00), the exterior entry queue alone takes 20 to 35 minutes. You must arrive at Parking P1 or P2 a minimum of **3 hours and 15 minutes before scheduled flight departure** (allowing 20 minutes for vehicle sign-off and deposit release, plus 2 hours and 55 minutes for terminal entry, check-in, police emigration stamps, and central security).\n\n**2. Refueling & Diesel AdBlue Forensics**:\nOver 85% of Moroccan rental fleets operate on Diesel (*Gasoil*), while specific urban automatic trims (such as the Peugeot 208 PureTech) utilize Unleaded Petrol (*Essence Sans Plomb*). Misfueling is entirely excluded from every insurance tier in Morocco; draining a contaminated high-pressure common-rail fuel injection system costs between 4,500 and 12,000 MAD (€420–€1,120).\n\nMost CMN contracts operate on a strict *Niveau-à-Niveau* (Same-to-Same, measured in eighths on the digital gauge) or Full-to-Full policy. If you return a car short by even one-eighth of a tank, agencies charge a punitive refueling tariff of 18 to 22 MAD per liter (compared to the regulated pump rate of approx. 12.80 MAD/L for Gasoil and 14.70 MAD/L for Sans Plomb), plus a 150 MAD administrative service fee.\n\n| Service Station Location | Distance to CMN P1/P2 | Operational Status & Forensic Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Afriquia Station (A3 Airport Spur / Nouaceur)** | 2.8 km before Terminal Loop | Open 24/7. Accepts CMI cards, though pump attendants prefer cash at night. |\n| **Shell Station (Bouskoura / A3 Southbound)** | 14.5 km before CMN Exit | Open 24/7. High-pressure tire air gauge & Quick-Wash facility (40 MAD). |\n| **TotalEnergies (Route de Nouaceur N9)** | 4.2 km from Terminal 2 | Open 06:00–23:00. Keep printed VAT receipt (*Ticket de Caisse*). |\n\n**3. Closing the Contract & Releasing the CMI Lock**:\nWhen handing keys back to the agent in P1 or P2, never leave the vehicle unattended with the keys on the dashboard or drop them in an unmanned box unless explicitly forced by a 03:00 AM unstaffed departure—and even then, record a complete 360-degree walkaround video plus a close-up of the illuminated odometer and fuel gauge showing the terminal roof in the background. For staffed returns, insist that the agent processes the **Annulation de Pré-autorisation** on the handheld CMI terminal and hands you the printed thermal receipt confirming the release before you walk toward the terminal departure doors.",
                "callout": {
                    "type": "tip",
                    "text": "If you drove down to Marrakech, Ouarzazate, or coastal Essaouira, spend 40 MAD (€3.70) for an exterior high-pressure rinse (Lavage Extérieur) at the Bouskoura highway service station before hitting CMN. Returning a car coated in red mud or coastal salt dust allows counter inspectors to trigger an 'Uninspected Bodywork Reserve' clause, locking your deposit for an extra 7 days pending an internal wash audit."
                }
            }
