                "content": "Driving out of Tangier requires navigating two radically different road infrastructures: the high-speed *Autoroutes du Maroc* (ADM) toll network heading south and east, and the notorious two-lane National Route 2 (N2) climbing into the Rif Mountains toward Tétouan, Chefchaouen, Al Hoceima, and Nador.\n\n**1. The ADM Motorway Network (A5 South & A4 East)**\n- **A5 Corridor (Tangier – Rabat – Casablanca):** Departs directly near TNG Airport. This is a pristine dual-carriageway rated for 120 km/h. If your vehicle is equipped with an electronic **Jawaz transponder tag** mounted behind the rearview mirror, use the dedicated yellow Jawaz lanes (marked with a 'J' overhead) at 30 km/h. If not, enter the green manual cash/card lanes.\n- **A4 Corridor (Tangier – Tanger Med Port):** The vital 54 km motorway linking the city to the main ferry port. Do not underestimate the crosswinds (*Chergui* and Atlantic gusts) near the Melloussa viaducts; they reach 80 km/h and actively push high-profile vehicles laterally.\n\n| ADM Highway Segment | Distance | Class 1 Toll (MAD / EUR) | Primary Strategic Hazards |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **TNG Airport to Asilah (A5)** | 38 km | 15 MAD (€1.40) | Sudden fog banks near Tahaddart |\n| **Tangier to Kenitra / Rabat (A5)**| 240 km | 92 MAD (€8.50) | Fixed average-speed radars at Larache |\n| **Tangier to Tanger Med Port (A4)**| 54 km | 27 MAD (€2.50) | Severe crosswinds & heavy HGV trucks |\n\n**2. The N2 & N13 Route to Tétouan and Chefchaouen**\nTo reach Chefchaouen (115 km, 2h15m drive from TNG Airport), do not take the coastal motorway. Exit TNG via the **N2 national road toward Tétouan**. While recently widened into a dual carriageway along several valleys, the N2 transitions into mountain switchbacks past Derdara as it merges onto the **N13**.\n\nHere, **Law 116-14 radar enforcement** is surgical. The Royal Gendarmerie deploys tripod-mounted laser speed cameras (*Cinémomètres Laser TruCAM*) hidden behind oleander bushes at the exact transition markers where the speed limit drops from 100 km/h to 80 km/h, and immediately to 60 km/h at rural commune entrances (*Entrées d'Agglomération* such as Hakama, Khemis Anjra, and Ben Karrich).\n\nUnder Moroccan law, speed radar tolerances are capped at a strict **10% margin (maximum 7 km/h)**. Clocking 68 km/h inside a 60 km/h village zone triggers an immediate **Class 3 Contravention**:\n- **Official Fine:** 300 MAD (€28)\n- **Immediate Cash Settlement Discount (Paiement Immédiat):** 150 MAD (€14) if paid in physical Dirham banknotes on the spot against an official numbered receipt (*Quittance à Souche*).\n\n**Vehicle Selection for the Rif Mountains:**\nIf traversing Tangier to Chefchaouen, Fes, or across the N2/N16 coastal Rocade Méditerranéenne to Al Hoceima and Nador, transmission and ground clearance dictate safety. For 2 passengers with cabin luggage, the `dacia-sandero-stepway` offers 174 mm of ground clearance to handle degraded asphalt shoulders, while the `renault-clio-5-auto` eliminates clutch fatigue during steep hill starts in Chefchaouen’s Outa el Hammam approaches. For 3 to 4 passengers with full suitcases, step up to the `hyundai-tucson-auto`; its higher torque band allows safe, decisive overtaking of slow agricultural trucks on short uphill passing zones.",
